{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"\nLet me analyze this task carefully.\n\nThe task SE-PE-01 is about grammar-guided node reduction (similar to Perses algorithm). I need to implement `solve(case)` that:\n\n1. Takes a parse tree with nodes having: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility\n2. Has required_symbols that must remain represented\n3. Must reduce the tree by removing nodes while preserving required symbols\n4. Rules:\n   - Never remove root, a required symbol node, or a node with retained children\n   - Consider deeper nodes before shallower nodes (process deepest first)\n   - Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\n   - Use node ID as stable tie-break at equal depth\n   - Return sorted retained IDs and actual removal order\n\nLet me think about the algorithm:\n\n1. Build the tree structure\n2. Process nodes from deepest to shallowest (deepest first), using node ID as tie-break at equal depth\n3. For each node (in this order), check if it can be removed:\n   - Node must be removable (removable=true)\n   - Node must not be root\n   - Node's symbol must not be in required_symbols... wait, let me re-read\n   \nWait, \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\"\n\nSo \"a required symbol\" means a node whose symbol is in required_symbols? Or the root node?\n\nLooking at the fixtures: required_symbols includes \"root\" and \"s1\". So nodes with symbol \"root\" or \"s1\" cannot be removed.\n\nAlso \"a node with retained children\" - so if a node has children that are still retained, it can't be removed.\n\n\"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" - this means after removing, all symbols in required_symbols must still appear in at least one retained node.\n\nLet me re-read the ordering: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes\" and \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\"\n\nSo the processing order is: sort by depth descending, then by node_id ascending (lexicographic?) as tie-break.\n\nLet me trace through case 000:\nNodes:\n- root: depth=0, removable=false, symbol=\"root\"\n- n1: depth=1, removable=false, symbol=\"s1\"\n- n2: depth=1, removable=true, symbol=\"s2\"\n- n3: depth=1, removable=false, symbol=\"s0\"\n- n4: depth=2, removable=true, symbol=\"s1\", parent=n2\n- n5: depth=2, removable=false, symbol=\"s2\", parent=n3\n- n6: depth=2, removable=true, symbol=\"s0\", parent=n1\n\nrequired_symbols: [\"root\", \"s1\"]\n\nProcessing order (deepest first, then by node ID):\nDepth 2: n4, n5, n6 (sorted by ID: n4, n5, n6)\nDepth 1: n1, n2, n3\nDepth 0: root\n\nFor each node in order, try to remove it:\n\nn4 (depth=2, removable=true, symbol=\"s1\"):\n- symbol \"s1\" is in required_symbols → can't remove (it's a required symbol)\n- Skip\n\nn5 (depth=2, removable=false, symbol=\"s2\"):\n- removable=false → can't remove\n- Skip\n\nn6 (depth=2, removable=true, symbol=\"s0\", parent=n1):\n- removable=true, symbol not in required_symbols\n- Check if it has retained children: n6 has no children → ok\n- Check if removing n6 keeps all required symbols represented:\n  - After removing n6, retained nodes include: root(root), n1(s1), n2(s2), n3(s0), n4(s1), n5(s2)\n  - required_symbols: \"root\" → present (root node), \"s1\" → present (n1, n4)\n  - All required symbols still present → can remove\n- Remove n6\n\nn1 (depth=1, removable=false, symbol=\"s1\"):\n- removable=false → can't remove\n\nn2 (depth=1, removable=true, symbol=\"s2\"):\n- removable=true, symbol not in required_symbols\n- Check retained children: n4 is a child of n2. Is n4 retained? Yes (it's still in retained set)\n- Has retained children → can't remove\n\nn3 (depth=1, removable=false, symbol=\"s0\"):\n- removable=false → can't remove\n\nroot (depth=0, removable=false, symbol=\"root\"):\n- removable=false → can't remove\n\nSo removed: [n6], retained: [root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5]\n\nWait, but that seems like not much reduction. Let me reconsider whether n4 is treated as a required symbol node.\n\n\"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\"\n\n\"a required symbol\" - I think this means a node whose symbol is listed in required_symbols. So n4 has symbol \"s1\" which is in required_symbols, so n4 cannot be removed.\n\nBut wait - could it mean that at least one node with each required symbol must remain? Let me re-read: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\"\n\nThese seem like two separate rules:\n1. Never remove a node if its symbol is a required_symbol (absolute prohibition)\n2. Tentatively remove a node only if after removal, all required symbols remain represented\n\nHmm, but if rule 1 is absolute, then rule 2 is a weaker condition (because if we can't remove required symbol nodes, we still need to check rule 2 for removable nodes whose symbol happens to not be required).\n\nWait, actually these might be the same concept expressed differently. Let me think again...\n\n\"Never remove the root, a required symbol\" - this could mean:\n- Never remove the root node\n- Never remove a node if its symbol is a required_symbol\n\nOR it could mean: \"a required symbol [node]\" = a node that is the sole representative of a required symbol.\n\nThe second interpretation aligns better with \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" - meaning you can remove a node with a required symbol as long as another node with that symbol still exists.\n\nLet me try interpretation 2: A node is a \"required symbol\" node (and thus cannot be removed) if removing it would leave some required symbol unrepresented.\n\nUnder interpretation 2:\nn4 has symbol \"s1\". Other nodes with \"s1\": n1. So if n4 is removed, \"s1\" is still represented by n1. So n4 could potentially be removed.\n\nBut wait, n4's removable=true, so...\n\nActually, rethinking: \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" might just be shorthand for the rule \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" These could be the same rule stated two ways.\n\nOr perhaps the rules are:\n1. Hard constraints: can't remove if removable=false, can't remove root, can't remove if has retained children\n2. Soft constraint (interestingness): can only remove if all required symbols remain\n\nLet me re-read: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\"\n\nI think \"a required symbol\" refers to a node whose symbol is in required_symbols. That's the clearest reading.\n\nBut then \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" is a separate, weaker condition.\n\nSo under this:\nn4 (symbol=\"s1\", required) → never remove\nn6 (symbol=\"s0\", not required, no children) → can we remove? \n- Not root, not required symbol, no retained children\n- After removal: still have root (symbol=root), n1 (s1), n2, n3, n4 (s1). Both \"root\" and \"s1\" still represented.\n- Remove n6.\n\nAfter removing n6, try n2:\n- symbol=\"s2\", not required, removable=true\n- Retained children of n2: n4 (still retained)\n- Has retained children → can't remove\n\nSo final result: removed=[n6], retained=[root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5]\n\nHmm, but that's a very small reduction. Let me think if n4 could be removed...\n\nIf \"required symbol\" means nodes with required symbols can NEVER be removed (regardless of other representatives), then n4 can't be removed, n2 can't be removed (has n4 as child), and only n6 can be removed.\n\nIf \"required symbol\" means the constraint is just about keeping representation, then:\n- n4 can be tentatively removed since n1 also has \"s1\"\n- After removing n4, check: root→\"root\"✓, s1→n1✓. OK.\n- Then n2: n4 is removed, so n2 has no retained children. n2's symbol is \"s2\" (not required). After removing n2: root✓, s1(n1)✓. OK. Remove n2.\n\nThis seems more consistent with a reduction algorithm like Perses.\n\nLet me look at the order again with interpretation 2 (symbol constraint = \"all required symbols remain after removal\"):\n\nProcessing order (depth desc, then node ID asc):\nn4, n5, n6, n1, n2, n3, root\n\nn4 (depth=2, removable=true, symbol=\"s1\"):\n- removable=true\n- Not root\n- No children\n- After removal, required symbols still represented? \"root\" → root node still there. \"s1\" → n1 still has symbol \"s1\". ✓\n- Remove n4\n\nn5 (depth=2, removable=false):\n- removable=false → skip\n\nn6 (depth=2, removable=true, symbol=\"s0\"):\n- removable=true, not root, no children\n- After removal: \"root\"→root✓, \"s1\"→n1✓. (n4 already removed)\n- Remove n6\n\nn1 (depth=1, removable=false):\n- Skip\n\nn2 (depth=1, removable=true, symbol=\"s2\"):\n- removable=true, not root\n- Retained children: n4 removed, so check remaining children. n4 is the only child of n2. n4 was removed. So n2 has no retained children.\n- After removal: \"root\"→root✓, \"s1\"→n1✓\n- Remove n2\n\nn3 (depth=1, removable=false):\n- Skip\n\nroot (depth=0, removable=false):\n- Skip\n\nFinal: removed=[n4, n6, n2], retained=[root, n1, n3, n5]\n\nHmm, but let me verify against the task description again. \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\"\n\nI now think \"a required symbol\" here specifically means a node that IS a required symbol - i.e., its symbol is one of the required symbols. Not \"the only node with that symbol\".\n\nBut if n1 (symbol=\"s1\") is non-removable anyway (removable=false), then the \"never remove required symbols\" rule would only additionally block removable nodes with required symbols.\n\nIn case 000, n4 (removable=true, symbol=\"s1\") would be blocked by \"never remove a required symbol\" under interpretation 1.\n\nBut \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" as a SEPARATE rule suggests that under interpretation 1, we might still need this second check.\n\nActually wait - what if \"never remove ... a required symbol\" is a shorthand and \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" is the actual implementation of that rule? In other words, \"a required symbol\" node = \"a node that is the sole remaining representative of a required symbol.\"\n\nThis makes the algorithm greedy: try to remove, check if all required symbols remain represented, if yes remove, if no keep.\n\nI'll go with interpretation 2 (the \"tentative\" check is the main mechanism for required symbols), as it makes the algorithm more interesting and consistent with Perses.\n\nLet me code this up:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    # Build node lookup and children map\n    node_map = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is not None:\n            children[n[\"parent\"]].append(n[\"id\"])\n    \n    # Processing order: deepest first, then by node ID (ascending) as tie-break\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    \n    retained = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes)\n    removed_order = []\n    \n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        \n        # Hard constraints\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        if node[\"parent\"] is None:  # root\n            continue\n        \n        # Check no retained children\n        has_retained_children = any(c in retained for c in children[nid])\n        if has_retained_children:\n            continue\n        \n        # Tentative removal: check all required symbols still represented\n        tentative_retained = retained - {nid}\n        symbols_remaining = set(node_map[rid][\"symbol\"] for rid in tentative_retained)\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(symbols_remaining):\n            continue\n        \n        # Remove\n        retained = tentative_retained\n        removed_order.append(nid)\n    \n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": sorted(list(retained))\n    }\n```\n\nWait, let me check: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\"\n- retained_node_ids: sorted (lexicographic? or by depth/position?)\n- removed_node_ids: actual removal order (the order in which nodes were removed)\n\n\"Return sorted retained IDs\" - I'll sort lexicographically.\n\nLet me trace through case 000 again with my code:\n\nNodes sorted by (-depth, id):\n- n4 (depth=2), n5 (depth=2), n6 (depth=2), n1 (depth=1), n2 (depth=1), n3 (depth=1), root (depth=0)\n\nInitial retained: {root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, n6}\n\nn4: removable=true, has parent(n2), children=[] \n- No retained children\n- Tentative retained = {root, n1, n2, n3, n5, n6}\n- Symbols: root(root), n1(s1), n2(s2), n3(s0), n5(s2), n6(s0) → {root, s1, s2, s0}\n- Required: {root, s1} ⊆ {root, s1, s2, s0} ✓\n- Remove n4. retained = {root, n1, n2, n3, n5, n6}, removed=[n4]\n\nn5: removable=false → skip\n\nn6: removable=true, parent=n1, children=[]\n- No retained children\n- Tentative: {root, n1, n2, n3, n5}\n- Symbols: root, s1, s2, s0, s2 → {root, s1, s2, s0}\n- Required: {root, s1} ✓\n- Remove n6. retained = {root, n1, n2, n3, n5}, removed=[n4, n6]\n\nn1: removable=false → skip\n\nn2: removable=true, parent=root\n- Children of n2: [n4]. n4 not in retained. No retained children.\n- Tentative: {root, n1, n3, n5}\n- Symbols: root, s1, s0, s2 → {root, s1, s0, s2}\n- Required: {root, s1} ✓\n- Remove n2. retained = {root, n1, n3, n5}, removed=[n4, n6, n2]\n\nn3: removable=false → skip\n\nroot: removable=false → skip (also parent is None)\n\nResult:\n- removed_node_ids: [\"n4\", \"n6\", \"n2\"]\n- retained_node_ids: sorted([\"root\", \"n1\", \"n3\", \"n5\"]) = [\"n1\", \"n3\", \"n5\", \"root\"]\n\nWait, lexicographic sort: \"n1\" \u003c \"n3\" \u003c \"n5\" \u003c \"root\". Yes.\n\nLet me also check case 001:\nNodes:\n- root: depth=0, removable=false, symbol=\"root\"\n- n1: depth=1, removable=false, symbol=\"s1\"\n- n2: depth=1, removable=true, symbol=\"s2\"\n- n3: depth=1, removable=false, symbol=\"s0\"\n- n4: depth=2, removable=true, symbol=\"s1\", parent=n2\n- n5: depth=2, removable=false, symbol=\"s2\", parent=n3\n- n6: depth=2, removable=true, symbol=\"s0\", parent=n1\n\nProcessing order: n4, n5, n6, n1, n2, n3, root\n\nn4: removable=true, no children\n- Tentative: {root,n1,n2,n3,n5,n6}\n- Symbols: root,s1,s2,s0,s2,s0 → required {root,s1} → s1 present (n1) ✓\n- Remove n4\n\nn5: removable=false → skip\n\nn6: removable=true, no children\n- Tentative: {root,n1,n2,n3,n5}\n- Symbols: root,s1,s2,s0,s2 → required {root,s1} ✓\n- Remove n6\n\nn1: removable=false → skip\n\nn2: removable=true, children=[n4], n4 not retained\n- Tentative: {root,n1,n3,n5}\n- Symbols: root,s1,s0,s2 → required {root,s1} ✓\n- Remove n2\n\nn3: removable=false → skip\nroot: skip\n\nResult: removed=[n4,n6,n2], retained=[n1,n3,n5,root]\n\nCase 002:\nSame structure, same result since structure is the same and the algorithm doesn't use utility values (at least not in my current implementation).\n\nWait, does utility play any role? Looking at the task description: \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\"\n\nThe candidate_artifact says: \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" - this implies the primary sort is by depth, tie-broken by node ID. No mention of utility in the ordering.\n\nI think utility might not be used in this specific task's algorithm. The algorithm is: process deepest nodes first (tie-break by ID), greedily remove if constraints are satisfied.\n\nActually, looking more carefully at all 3 cases - they have the same tree structure and same required_symbols, just different utility values. If utility doesn't factor in, all 3 would produce the same result. That seems intentional - the fixture is testing the structural algorithm.\n\nMy implementation looks correct. Let me finalize it and format as JSON.\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    # Build node lookup and children map\n    node_map = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is not None:\n            children[n[\"parent\"]].append(n[\"id\"])\n    \n    # Processing order: deepest first, then by node ID ascending as tie-break\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    \n    retained = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes)\n    removed_order = []\n    \n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        \n        # Hard constraint: must be removable\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        \n        # Hard constraint: never remove root (parent is None)\n        if node[\"parent\"] is None:\n            continue\n        \n        # Hard constraint: cannot remove if has retained children\n        has_retained_children = any(c in retained for c in children[nid])\n        if has_retained_children:\n            continue\n        \n        # Tentative removal check: all required symbols must remain represented\n        tentative_retained = retained - {nid}\n        symbols_remaining = {node_map[rid][\"symbol\"] for rid in tentative_retained}\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(symbols_remaining):\n            continue\n        \n        # Commit removal\n        retained = tentative_retained\n        removed_order.append(nid)\n    \n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": sorted(list(retained))\n    }\n```\n\nNow I need to format this as a JSON string with proper escaping and wrap in the submission envelope.\n","type":"thinking"},{"text":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required_symbols = set(case[\\\"required_symbols\\\"])\\n\\n    # Build node lookup and children map\\n    node_map = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n[\\\"parent\\\"] is not None:\\n            children[n[\\\"parent\\\"]].append(n[\\\"id\\\"])\\n\\n    # Processing order: deepest first, then by node ID ascending as stable tie-break\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\\\"depth\\\"], n[\\\"id\\\"]))\\n\\n    retained = set(n[\\\"id\\\"] for n in nodes)\\n    removed_order = []\\n\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node[\\\"id\\\"]\\n\\n        # Hard constraint: node must be flagged removable\\n        if not node[\\\"removable\\\"]:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Hard constraint: never remove root (parent is None)\\n        if node[\\\"parent\\\"] is None:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Hard constraint: cannot remove a node that still has retained children\\n        if any(c in retained for c in children[nid]):\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Interestingness oracle: tentatively remove and verify all required\\n        # symbols remain represented among the retained nodes\\n        tentative_retained = retained - {nid}\\n        symbols_remaining = {node_map[rid][\\\"symbol\\\"] for rid in tentative_retained}\\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(symbols_remaining):\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Commit removal\\n        retained = tentative_retained\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_order,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": sorted(retained)\\n    }\\n\"}","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_01ytlNTIr7Mryqht6WxnyKIV","model":"claude-opus-4-7","role":"assistant","stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2422},"cache_creation_input_tokens":2422,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":38,"output_tokens":5562,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":5100},"service_tier":"standard"}}